# canonical-source-leading-group: pins the canonical source of a Text
# Pattern whose first element is a nested group. Splicing `<4 digits>` first
# gives `< <4 digits>, "x">`, and splicing it alone gives `< <4 digits>>`,
# with one space after the opening `<`, so neither reads back as Bytes
# (spec chapter 11, The canonical source of a Text Pattern). The display
# form in `vars` is read with chapter 2's Text Pattern grammar, and a
# literal written the same way is equal to the spliced pattern, since
# equality compares canonical sources (chapter 3, Text Patterns). The
# values are pinned. The programs of unit, first and only have 5, 6 and 5
# instructions, so their sizes are 56, 64 and 56. With same's 8, state
# is 184. The two make-pattern instructions cost 32 + 30 Fuel and
# allocate 64 + 56 = 120; dispatch and the other instructions cost 19,
# giving 81 Fuel (chapter 8, Cost Model 0).

> load leading identity=7ed516582c0898e42e3e7bb2b863b3a0188f43cf69ed0f4b82376dd96113ef4b
> deliver d1 to=leading message=go
> pump clock=2026-09-30T09:00:00Z
seg leading/r1 start delivery=d1 handler=go clause=1 fuel=81 alloc=120 state=184 end=return
run leading/r1 outcome=completed delivery=d1 handler=go fuel=81 alloc=120
pumped state=idle fuel=81

> vars
vars leading unit=<4 digits> first=< <4 digits>, "x"> only=< <4 digits>> same=true
